\title{Motivation}
\date{2025-12-14T02:35:59Z}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Consider the following two problems:}

\ol{
  \li{30 people each buy a ticket to see the heffalump. The owner pays $12 for food and ends up with $33 in profit. How much does one ticket cost?}
  \li{In what circumstances does the following loop \em{stop}?
\pre\verb<<<|
while (tickets < 30 or (!heffalumps.isEmpty() and not(count > 5))):
  ...<<<
  }
}

\p{How would you solve these?  Different students give different
answers, but if you are like many, you would solve the first one by
writing down an algebraic equation and solving it, and the second one
by talking about it and thinking hard.  The first problem definitely
seems easier.}

\p{It turns out that people used to solve algebra problems like the
first using natural language and thinking really hard.  Formal
notation is a superpower! Our goal is to allow you to solve the second
problem using a formal language too.}

\p{XXX Another motivation: how do we rigorously \em{prove} a
mathematical statement?  How do we know what details we need to
include, and what counts as a correct argument?  Language of
propositional logic can help with this too!}
